====Interjection====
{{en-intj}}

# {{alternative spelling of|en|nyet}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=w:Odette Tchernine|chapter=The Russians Investigate|title=The Snowman and Company|location=London|publisher=[[w:Robert Hale (publishers)|Robert Hale]]|page=59|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/snowmancompany0000odet/page/59/mode/1up|oclc=13978994|passage=Or again, the Russian Government may now take a poor view of publicizing rumours of Snowmen being sighted within their own frontiers. They may prefer to say '''''Net''''' to the prospect of dealing with inquiring foreigners who might ask for permission to go rampaging for footprints and sightings all over their mountains.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1971|journal=Bulletin|location=Munich|publisher=Institute for the Study of the USSR|page=29|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=K9IfAAAAMAAJ&q=%22say+net%22|oclc=1463509281|passage=Communist propaganda has created for Russia a reputation of a “peace-loving” power which it values. It cannot, therefore, afford to say '''''net''''' to any and every Western proposal.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=w:Colin Thubron|chapter=Leningrad|title=Among the Russians|location=London|publisher=[[w:Heinemann (publisher)|Heinemann]]|page=74|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/amongrussians0000thub/page/74/mode/1up|isbn=0 434 77986 5|passage=Then as now this impersonal vastness was the public face of Russia. Its voice, if it had one, intoned an unmistakable '''''Net'''''. And all the canals and looking-glass mansions, in their courtly but firm way, were saying '''''Net''''' too.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=William Minor|chapter=The Ukraine, in Joy and Sorrow|title=Unzipped Souls: A Jazz Journey through the Soviet Union|location=Philadlephia, Pa.|publisher=w:Temple University Press|section=part 3 (Leningrad and the Republics)|page=160|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/unzippedsoulsjaz00mino/page/160/mode/1up|isbn=1-56639-324-8|passage=He asked me what nationality I was, and I replied, “American.” / “'''Net''', '''net''',” he cried. “What nationality?”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|editor=Tālavs Jundzis|title={{lang|lv|Baltijas valstis likten̦griežos: Politiskas, ekonomiskas un tiesiskas starptautiskās sadarbības problēmas uz XXI gadu simten̦a sliekšn̦a}}{{nb...|Rakstu krājums Senatora Augusta Lēbera piemin̦ai un sakarā ar profesora Dītricha Andreja Lēbera 75 gadu jubileju}}|worklang=lv|location=Riga|publisher=Latvijas Zinātn̦u akadēmija|page=454|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=umm0AAAAIAAJ&q=%22to+border+deal%22|isbn=9984-9085-7-7|passage=Sal. Russia says ‘'''net'''’ to border deal /&nbsp;/ The Baltic Times. – 1997.}}
